  <h2>The Problem</h2>
  <p>
    Light is the most powerful circadian signal your body receives. The spectrum, timing,
    and intensity of the light entering your eyes dictates melatonin production, cortisol
    curves, testosterone release timing, and metabolic rate throughout the day. Your biology
    evolved calibrating to sunlight. Modern indoor lighting delivers the wrong spectrum at
    the wrong times — all day, every day.
  </p>
  <p>
    Standard LED and fluorescent bulbs emit a blue-dominant spectrum that tells the brain
    it's high noon — at 10pm, while you're trying to wind down. Melatonin production
    suppresses. Sleep onset delays. Sleep architecture degrades. And most men have no idea
    their light environment is the primary culprit.
  </p>
  <p>
    The inverse is equally important: most men indoors all morning are never getting the
    full-spectrum sunlight exposure that anchors the circadian clock. The result is a system
    with no strong signal at either end — no powerful morning anchor, no clean evening
    drop-off. The clock drifts, and everything it controls drifts with it.
  </p>

  <h2>The Insulting Behaviors</h2>
  <ul class="insult-list">
    <li>Blue-spectrum LED bulbs in the bedroom and living room active all evening</li>
    <li>No morning outdoor sun exposure — the primary circadian anchor never fires</li>
    <li>Screens (phone, TV, laptop) within 60 minutes of sleep</li>
    <li>Overhead fluorescent or LED lighting in home office throughout the day with no natural light</li>
    <li>Sunglasses during the first 30 minutes after waking — blocks the signal before it reaches the suprachiasmatic nucleus</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="section-eliminate">Eliminate the Insulting Behavior</h2>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Step 1 — Morning Sun on Your Eyes</h3>
      <p>5–10 minutes of direct, unfiltered morning sunlight within 30 minutes of waking. No glasses. This single behavior has the highest ROI of any circadian intervention.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Step 2 — Swap Evening Bulbs</h3>
      <p>Replace bedroom and living-room LED bulbs with warm-spectrum (2200–2700K) or red-spectrum bulbs. After sundown, your biology needs amber and red — not blue and white.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Step 3 — Blue-Blockers After Sundown</h3>
      <p>Amber-lens blue-blocking glasses worn from sundown until sleep. Eliminates screen-driven melatonin suppression without eliminating screen use entirely.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Step 4 — Red Light Therapy 10–20 Min/Day</h3>
      <p>660nm and 850nm red and near-infrared light panels support mitochondrial function, tissue recovery, and mood. Used morning or midday — not within 2 hours of sleep.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

